    <p class="note">[Rows illustrative — canonical matrix (Q6 committee) supplies final claims. Workspace/silicon rows describe Essentials & Pro; Launch derates (published caps). "Dongle class" = ClickShare Buttons, InstaShow, VIA buttons, HDMI transmitter kits.]</p>
    <div class="mdna reveal"><h3>Our take: the dongle era</h3>
      <p>Dongles won a real war: they killed the cable at the moment when nothing else could. But every dongle sold today is a bet that the meeting still lives on one laptop and the room still needs hardware handed around to see it. Neither is true anymore. The browser ended the hand-out; the workspace ended the single mirror; hybrid ended the idea that pixels-to-display is the whole job.</p>
      <div class="concede">Honest concession: a point-to-point dongle kit works with zero network involvement. For an air-gapped boardroom or a rental space with no IT, a button is the right answer. Everywhere else you are buying a second hardware fleet to hand around.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="two reveal">
      <div class="card"><h3>Compare by name instead</h3><p>Pick a specific vendor (Barco, Airtame, ScreenBeam, and the rest) in the compare hub.</p><a href="/compare/hub">Open the compare hub →</a></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>The other category question</h3><p>Locked VTC room systems: a per-room platform license, one vendor by default, and a design where the default motion is start-a-call &mdash; which is why Barco now sells ClickShare Hub to bolt wireless presenting onto a Teams Room, at a second hardware purchase and a second licence.</p><a href="/compare/mtr">Why not a VTC room system? →</a></div>
    </div>
